The demo may add explicitly configured extensions for domains not covered by this taxonomy, for example:

informatique:
- pc portable
- ordinateur
- ordinateur portable
- laptop

Taxonomy matching rules:

1. Try configured category and subcategory values first.
2. Use configured aliases and accent-insensitive administrator rules where configured.
3. Use category → subcategory relationships.
4. If multiple categories match the same subcategory, do not guess without context.
5. If the value is not supported, return null rather than hallucinating.
6. A new taxonomy entry may be added through configuration or the demo editor; do not silently invent and persist taxonomy values.
7. If an LLM proposes an unknown category/subcategory, accept it only when it passes configured validation and confidence policy.

6. Domain parsing rules
----------------------

6.1 General rules

- Never hallucinate.
- Never invent a brand.
- Never invent a reference.
- Never infer material without explicit evidence or authorized configured inference rules.
- Preserve product terminology.
- Prefer explicit evidence over inference.
- Prefer configured catalog knowledge over generic assumptions.
- Preserve dimensions and units.
- Keep references separate from dimensions and models.
- Detect ambiguity rather than guessing.
- Keep the parser independent of search and recommendation systems.

6.2 Abbreviation

Add an abbreviation field.

The abbreviation is normally the first term of the description when:

- It is an explicitly configured abbreviation or alias; or
- It is a short all-capital alphabetic code, such as COU, ROB, or RAC; or
- A validated LLM extraction supplies it with confidence above the configured threshold.

Do not classify normal long words such as ROBINET or COUDE as abbreviations unless explicitly configured.

The abbreviation rule must be configurable per field. The administrator must be able to customize instructions, examples, allowed values, aliases, regex rules, and confidence thresholds.

6.3 Collisage / packaging prefix

Add a collisage field.

The collisage is extracted only from the beginning of the description when it represents a packaging or selling unit, such as:

- 1pcs
- 1pc
- 1kg
- 1kgrs
- jeux
- jeu
- 1dz
- 1m
- rlx
- lot
- carton
- paquet

Do not classify the product term PC in "pc portable" as collisage. An unnumbered PC must be available for name extraction.

Collisage values may be normalized to lowercase using a configured normalization rule.

6.4 Quality

The quality field must recognize configured values including:

- standard
- premium
- 1ch
- 3ch
- original
- chinoise

Configured aliases must include examples such as:

- 1 ch -> 1ch
- 3 ch -> 3ch
- originale -> original
- chinois -> chinoise

6.5 Made-in / origin

Add a madein field.

Recognize explicit origin markers:

- Made in
- Madein
- Origine
- Pays
- Country
- Provenance

Recognize explicit origin/status values:

- locale
- local
- importation
- import
- importé
- importée

Recognize configurable country-code aliases including:

- dz -> Algérie
- tn -> Tunisie
- fr -> France
- it -> Italie
- usa -> USA
- us -> USA
- cn -> Chine
- china -> Chine
- chine -> Chine

Do not infer madein from the brand, language, or product category.

Examples:

"robinet 1ch made in dz"

must produce:

quality: "1ch"
madein: "Algérie"

"robinet original origine fr"

must produce:

quality: "original"
madein: "France"

Avoid regexes that interpret original as the beginning of the word origine.

6.6 Dimensions

Recognize and preserve:

- 1/2
- 3/4
- M8
- 110x50
- 110x3.2
- 4x35
- 110mm
- 4m
- 45deg
- 45°

Standalone numeric values remain ambiguous unless authorized by context.

Examples:

- "ABC 110" must not arbitrarily become dims: ["110"]. It should remain ambiguous and use the resolver/tool policy.
- "Tube PVC 110" may extract dims: ["110"] because tube is configured dimensional context.
- "Coude PVC 110" may extract dims: ["110"] because coude is configured dimensional context.
- "1pcs pvc coude 110mm 45deg gris" must extract dims: ["110mm", "45deg"].

Only normalize multiplication x when it occurs between numeric values. Do not merge words ending in x, for example JEUX Coude.

6.7 PC portable example

The example configuration must support:

Input:

pc portable hp pavillon dv5 core duo

Expected result:

{
  "category": "informatique",
  "subcategory": "pc portable",
  "name": "pc portable",
  "abbreviation": null,
  "collisage": null,
  "model": "DV5",
  "brand": "HP",
  "serie": "Pavilion",
  "quality": null,
  "madein": null,
  "material": null,
  "dims": [],
  "color": null,
  "ref": null,
  "other": ["core duo"]
}

Required configuration:

- Brand HP with alias hp.
- Series Pavilion with aliases pavilion and pavillon.
- Model extraction regex for DV5-like values, normalized to uppercase.
- pc portable configured as name and taxonomy value.
- pc must not be treated as collisage.
- contiguous unsupported words such as core duo should remain one other phrase where possible.

7. Configuration system
-----------------------

The configuration must support:

- brands
- brand aliases
- series
- series aliases
- categories
- subcategories
- category/subcategory relationships
- allowed values
- aliases
- field-specific descriptions
- field-specific LLM instructions
- field-specific examples
- field extraction regexes
- capture groups
- context terms
- normalization rules
- validation rules
- inference permissions
- field confidence thresholds
- global confidence thresholds
- ambiguity policies
- retry limits
- tool-call limits
- timeout limits

Example field configuration:

fields: {
  ref: {
    kind: "string",
    description: "Product reference / manufacturer reference.",
    instructions: [
      "Usually contains letters and numbers.",
      "May contain hyphens or slashes.",
      "Prefer explicit REF, Réf, or Reference markers.",
      "Do not invent a reference."
    ],
    examples: ["RA12", "ABC-120", "REF12345"],
    extract: {
      patterns: ["\\b(?:REF|RÉF|REFERENCE)\\s*[:#]?\\s*([A-Z0-9][A-Z0-9/_-]*)"],
      flags: "i",
      captureGroup: 1
    }
  },
  dims: {
    kind: "stringArray",
    description: "Physical dimensions, connection sizes, diameters, lengths, widths, and angles.",
    instructions: [
      "Preserve numeric values and units.",
      "Recognize 1/2, 3/4, M8, 110x50, 110mm, 45deg, and 45°.",
      "Do not confuse references with dimensions."
    ],
    examples: ["1/2", "M8", "110x3.2", "110mm", "45deg", "4m"],
    extract: {
      contextTerms: ["tube", "tuyau", "coude", "dn", "diam", "diamètre", "dimension", "longueur", "largeur", "hauteur", "ø"]
    }
  },
  madein: {
    kind: "string",
    description: "Country, local/import status, or origin code.",
    instructions: [
      "Use explicit Made in, Origine, Pays, Country, Provenance, locale, importation, or configured country codes only.",
      "Do not infer origin from the brand."
    ]
  }
}

8. Extraction evidence and confidence
--------------------------------------

Internally maintain:

interface FieldResult<T> {
  value: T | null;
  confidence: number;
  source:
    | "explicit"
    | "normalized"
    | "catalog"
    | "inferred"
    | "tool";
  ambiguous: boolean;
}

Configurable thresholds:

confidence: {
  autoAccept: 0.90,
  requireTool: 0.75,
  rejectBelow: 0.50
}

Behavior:

- confidence >= autoAccept: accept.
- requireTool <= confidence < autoAccept: validate or use a tool.
- confidence < rejectBelow: null / unresolved.
- ambiguous=true: use the configured ambiguity policy.

9. Tool calling
---------------

Implement:

interface ParserTool {
  name: string;
  description: string;
  inputSchema?: unknown;
  execute(input: unknown): Promise<unknown>;
}

Implement a ToolRegistry with register, replace, unregister, list, definitions, and execute methods.

The parser must dynamically expose registered tools to the LLM.

Implement a built-in resolver:

resolveProductTerm({
  term: "Pro",
  context: "Somatherm",
  candidateFields: ["serie", "quality", "model"]
})

Possible responses:

{
  "status": "resolved",
  "field": "serie",
  "value": "Pro",
  "confidence": 0.98
}

or:

{
  "status": "ambiguous",
  "candidates": [
    { "field": "serie", "value": "Pro" },
    { "field": "quality", "value": "Pro" }
  ]
}

Use tools only when necessary. Do not call a tool for every high-confidence product.

Bound all calls with:

- maxRetries
- maxToolCalls
- timeoutMs

Tool failures must result in controlled warnings and unresolved fields, never a parser crash.

10. LLM architecture
--------------------

Implement:

interface LLMProvider {
  readonly modelName?: string;
  generateStructured(request: StructuredGenerationRequest): Promise<unknown>;
  generateWithTools(request: ToolGenerationRequest): Promise<LLMToolResponse>;
}

Implement QwenProvider as an adapter for an injected OpenAI-compatible local chat-completions endpoint. Do not hard-code Ollama or any cloud provider.

The provider must support:

- injectable endpoint
- injectable model
- optional API key
- optional headers
- injectable fetch implementation for tests
- temperature 0
- structured JSON schema
- OpenAI-compatible tool calls
- timeout handling

The parser must remain usable without an LLM through the deterministic catalog parser fallback.

11. Prompt architecture
-----------------------

Implement PromptBuilder. Do not create one giant hard-coded prompt.

Build prompts dynamically from:

- system instructions
- generated product schema
- field descriptions
- field instructions
- field examples
- category taxonomy
- subcategory taxonomy
- brand catalog
- series catalog
- normalization rules
- available tools
- raw product input
- normalized product input

The prompt must tell the model:

- extract only supported information
- never invent values
- return null and [] for missing values
- preserve dimensions and units
- distinguish references from dimensions/models
- flag ambiguity
- use tools only when necessary
- return structured output only
- never return chain-of-thought

12. Validation pipeline
-----------------------

Use this pipeline:

Raw text
  -> pre-normalization
  -> dynamic prompt creation
  -> LLM extraction or deterministic fallback
  -> JSON parsing
  -> Zod schema validation
  -> business validation
  -> tool validation/resolution
  -> deterministic field normalization
  -> final schema validation
  -> ParsedProduct result

Malformed LLM output must cause a bounded retry or deterministic fallback, never an unhandled crash.

13. Public API
--------------

Implement:

const parser = new ProductParser({
  config,
  llm: optionalProvider,
  tools: optionalTools,
  debug: false
});

const result = await parser.parse(description, {
  debug: true,
  includeFieldDetails: true
});

const batch = await parser.parseBatch(descriptions, {
  concurrency: 4
});

Return:

{
  data: ParsedProduct,
  metadata: {
    confidence: number,
    toolsUsed: string[],
    warnings: string[],
    processingTimeMs: number,
    modelName?: string,
    rawInput: string,
    normalizedInput: string,
    fieldConfidence?: Record<string, unknown>,
    debug?: {
      rawInput,
      normalizedInput,
      llmRequests,
      llmResponses,
      toolCalls,
      validationErrors,
      fieldDecisions,
      finalOutput,
      timings
    }
  }
}

14. HTML demo
-------------

Create a professional standalone development demo with inline CSS and no external runtime dependencies.

The demo must include:

- product description textarea
- Parse Product button
- predefined test cases
- JSON output panel
- field-by-field table
- confidence indicators
- source indicators
- ambiguity indicators
